# A method's own named Structure is built each time its declaration runs (OPUS-Q54-CONTINUE-20260929-14 2b; book §9:
# a structural expression constructs its value when execution reaches it): the first call writes S\v 9 and returns
# it, the second call's S is a new one, v 4 -- 94.  A Structure built once and kept in the method's slot would give 99.
fn: m (int: k) int
    S:
        int: v 4
    if: k = 1
        S\v: 9
    int: out 0
    out: S\v
    return: out
end: m
int: a (m: 1)
int: b (m: 0)
int: r (a * 10 + b)
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: r; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
